      "name": "IT Relocation Operations",
      "description": "IT relocation operations: servers, racks, workstations, network equipment and peripherals moved as a planned, labelled sequence, as part of commercial and office relocations. The published sequence runs: survey and quote — the plan, not the truck, is agreed first in the Detailed Project form, and the flat rate is quoted in writing before move day; inventory and labelling — every item is inventoried and labelled before anything is unplugged, so pieces arrive in the order the team needs them; protection — sensitive equipment travels padded, wrapped and strapped, with custom crating for the pieces that need it; the heavy-rack survey — racks and cabinets in the 1,500–3,500 lb band are surveyed under SIR Structural Integrity Relocation and the KIM-GS Protocol with an L/360 floor check, like any high-mass asset; the sequenced move itself, transported strapped and padded in the planned order; placement at the destination in the labelled order; and verification — equipment placed and verified at the destination. Industry practice around that chain, labeled domain only: serial numbers, rack positions and cable photographs recorded at inventory; waves run least-critical first; network and server equipment brought online first at the destination; desk tests for power, network, dock, monitors, phone and printer; documentation updated at close-out. Not published and not claimed for IT relocation: who performs shutdown, unplugging and reconnection — the published page states equipment is inventoried and labelled before anything is unplugged and does not assign the unplug itself to either side; backup, rollback or close-out scope; any named system, platform or vendor; any certification or clearance; any named data-center facility; any IT-specific price, service-level commitment or downtime commitment; branch-specific IT dispatch; handling for racks below 1,500 lbs beyond the published sequence.",
